WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:20.190
Latin America is, perhaps, the region with
the least military ambition in the world…

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:24.180
their governments spend relatively very little
on defense, and in general, with some exceptions,

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:29.610
creating large and powerful armies is not
a priority.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:36.010
Despite this, the subject of the Armed Forces
has been a very controversial issue in this

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:37.310
region for years.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:38.310
And...

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:42.739
No wonder, because the second half of the
20th century wasn’t exactly a quiet time

00:00:42.739 --> 00:00:43.890
for Latin America.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:48.440
Throughout this period, and especially in
the 1960s and 1970s, this region was one of

00:00:48.440 --> 00:01:00.780
the territories in the world which was most
affected by military force.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:24.150
In 

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:27.500
these years, practically every country in
the region participated in what became a kind

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:32.220
of macabre tradition of coups, military groups
and, of course, plenty of repression.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:02:04.320
For example, let's take a look at 1978...

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.880
In that year, among other countries, Argentina,
Bolivia, Brazil, Chile, Ecuador, Paraguay,

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.530
Peru, Cuba, Nicaragua, Honduras, El Salvador,
Panama and even the peaceful Uruguay were

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.330
under a military dictatorship.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:19.730
And forgive me if I left any out... as you
can see its a pretty long list.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:25.531
Dictators of every kind existed: left, right,
pro-US, anti-US, harder, softer... but they

00:02:25.531 --> 00:02:27.620
all had a common element: repression.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:37.420
And yes, in the framework of the Cold War
and block struggles, many of these dictatorships

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:42.200
were supported by the United States under
the framework of what was later known as Operation

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.200
Condor.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.130
A sinister intelligence and collaboration
plan forged between the military dictatorships

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:52.980
of Videla’s Argentina, Pinochet’s Chile,
Alfredo Stroessner’s Paraguay, Joao Figueiredo’s

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.010
Brazil and Bordaberry’s Uruguay.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:01.160
All aided by the CIA in order to achieve a
single objective: persecute and annihilate

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:08.450
the political opposition, especially the leftist
groups that were supported by the Soviet regime.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:09.770
Of course...

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:14.970
the dictatorships forged under the other political
color weren’t any better, not at all...

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:20.860
Considering this, it isn’t hard to imagine
why the Armed Forces issue has been so controversial.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:25.740
Well, more than 30 years have passed since
this dark period... and in this video we’ll

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.020
aim to answer several questions:

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.870
What is the current state of Latin America’s
armies?

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:32.580
What functions do they have?

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:36.230
Do they pose any risk to these countries’
democracy and freedom?

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:42.840
Well, let’s have a look…

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.099
ARMIES IN LATIN AMERICA

00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:49.500
Before we begin, and just out of curiosity,
let me ask you a question:

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:54.349
Which do you think are the strongest, most
powerful armies in Latin America?

00:03:54.349 --> 00:03:59.709
Well, this is always quite subjective, but
according to the Global FirePower Index 2017

00:03:59.709 --> 00:04:04.770
(an indicator that attempts to measure which
armies are more powerful under different parameters).

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:06.920
These are the top 5 in Latin America:

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:11.190
The first place may not be surprising, Brazil,
the greatest regional power.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:17.549
It is followed, in this order, by Mexico,
Argentina, Peru and Colombia.

00:04:17.549 --> 00:04:22.419
The truth is that Latin America is the region
with the smallest budget dedicated to defense.

00:04:22.419 --> 00:04:28.770
In 2016, it was just over 1% of its GDP, about
$67 billion.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:33.139
This expenditure is much lower than the global
average, especially because Latin American

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:38.819
armies tend to have quite a high number of
soldiers.

00:04:38.819 --> 00:04:45.520
To get an idea, in total the region has over
1,730,000 soldiers...

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:51.680
And of course, the combination of a low budget
with many soldiers, leads to outdated armies

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:54.199
with lots of operational problems.

00:04:54.199 --> 00:04:59.930
Let's take a look at the most important army,
the Brazilian army.

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:06.030
Brazil spends $23.7 billion every year...
which sounds like a lot, right?

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:11.400
Well, the problem is that it has more than
340,000 soldiers, which means that three-quarters

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.990
of the budget goes to salaries and pensions.

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:19.839
If to this we add basic supplies, then…
the money left for equipment is very, very

00:05:19.839 --> 00:05:22.229
low.

00:05:22.229 --> 00:05:27.080
And in the rest of Latin America, ratios are
similar...

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:32.530
on average, 70% of the money goes directly
to salaries....

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:48.789
In other words, Latin American armies usually
have a lot of soldiers and very little equipment...

00:05:48.789 --> 00:05:49.810
LOOKING INWARDS

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:56.259
So, if we talk about armies, wars, and conflicts
between countries, we could say that Latin

00:05:56.259 --> 00:05:59.710
America is one of the most peaceful regions
in the world.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:04.409
The last armed conflict occurred in 1995 in
what was later known as the Cenepa War, which

00:06:04.409 --> 00:06:13.610
involved Ecuador and Peru... even though no
formal declaration of war was ever made.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:17.639
Since then, a trend has begun that has made
Latin America a benchmark:

00:06:17.639 --> 00:06:21.699
Every time there is a conflict between countries,
instead of threatening each other with bombs

00:06:21.699 --> 00:06:25.439
and cannons, those involved resort to international
justice.

00:06:25.439 --> 00:06:29.719
This is what is currently happening, for example,
with the conflict between Bolivia and Chile

00:06:29.719 --> 00:06:34.119
over the seafront - a conflict that is being
resolved at the International Court of Justice

00:06:34.119 --> 00:06:35.119
in The Hague.

00:06:35.119 --> 00:06:40.400
"Bolivia replies to the lawsuit against Chile
at The Hague Court for sea access”.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:41.400
BBC

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:42.400
However...

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.350
Internally things are completely different.

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:48.619
We could say that Latin America has the record
for being the most peaceful region and at

00:06:48.619 --> 00:06:50.860
the same time, the most violent one....

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:57.830
What I mean here, is that between countries
there is no serious threat of war... but the

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:04.899
situation is different if we talk about internal
violence…

00:07:04.899 --> 00:07:13.939
So... because apparently all "enemies" are
at home, Latin American military forces focus

00:07:13.939 --> 00:07:15.479
on at-home tasks…

00:07:15.479 --> 00:07:20.249
such as confronting guerrillas, drug traffickers,
or dealing with just violence in general...

00:07:20.249 --> 00:07:23.930
In fact, in the region, it is becoming increasingly
common for military forces to replace the

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:26.699
police in security and surveillance tasks.

00:07:26.699 --> 00:07:30.690
The problem here is that evidence shows that
soldiers aren’t really good at playing the

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:31.690
role of police…

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:38.490
THE 
INSUFFICIENT SOLUTION

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:45.020
Contrary to what we might think at first,
crime rates in Brazil or Mexico, far from

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:49.629
decreasing, have increased since soldiers
began to participate in citizen security.

00:07:49.629 --> 00:07:55.309
In fact, the 5 most dangerous cities in the
world, Caracas, Acapulco, San Pedro Sula,

00:07:55.309 --> 00:07:58.759
Central District and Victoria, have a military
presence.

00:07:58.759 --> 00:08:04.969
And that's not all.

00:08:04.969 --> 00:08:09.330
Power abuse, extrajudicial detentions, or
collateral victims, have also increased with

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:14.050
military presence, which further favors and
feeds a culture of violence…

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:21.689
Something that is unfortunately far too widespread
in the region nowadays.

00:08:21.689 --> 00:08:27.499
The thing is that soldiers are forces trained
for war, to eliminate the enemy, not to impose

00:08:27.499 --> 00:08:28.990
order and the rule of law.

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:33.700
To achieve this, what is needed are detectives,
courts and judges, local police forces, and

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:34.920
citizen collaboration…

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:35.920
Not the military.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:43.769
We can take a look at what has happened, for
example, in Mexico.

00:08:43.769 --> 00:08:48.810
As soon as he became president, in December
2006, Felipe Calderón declared a war on drugs

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:50.560
and placed the army on the streets.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:55.990
Since then, not only has narcotics trafficking
not decreased, homicide rates have tripled.

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:58.310
The army is simply not enough...

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:02.519
They can, of course, lend a hand in very serious
cases, but they cannot be a police force…

00:09:02.519 --> 00:09:03.759
It simply doesn’t work.

00:09:03.759 --> 00:09:08.161
Yes, I know that corruption in the police
is a problem, but this isn’t fixed by simply

00:09:08.161 --> 00:09:09.870
resorting to using the army.

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:12.339
This actually leads to two other major problems:

00:09:12.339 --> 00:09:14.420
The first one is political risk.

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:15.470
Consider this:

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:18.649
"Latin America’s armed forces must be anti-imperialist"
Evo Morales.

00:09:18.649 --> 00:09:21.290
Well, what do you want me to say?

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:25.420
To me, seeing politicians using military forces
to defend their political ideas, whatever

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:28.690
they may be, seems very, very dangerous.

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:31.959
Using the power of force is always going to
be very tempting...

00:09:31.959 --> 00:09:35.850
This is what has happened, for example, in
Venezuela, where the Armed Forces have become

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:41.860
something like the Praetorian Guard for Nicolás
Maduro’s Bolivarian regime.

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:47.589
And if that wasn’t enough, there is also
the corruption problem...

00:09:47.589 --> 00:09:52.149
See, the military industry is an opaque industry
with huge contracts...

00:09:52.149 --> 00:09:54.940
two basic ingredients for corruption.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:57.970
Just see what happened in 2010 in the Dominican
Republic:

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:03.510
"In 2010 a major corruption scandal affected
the Dominican Armed Forces due to the fraudulent

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:07.910
purchase of 8 Super Tucanos aircraft from
the Brazilian company Embraer.

00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:12.710
It is suspected that 87 million dollars remained
in the hands of members of the country’s

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:14.449
political and military hierarchy."

00:10:14.449 --> 00:10:18.350
LILIAN BOBEA, professor at Bentley University
in Massachusetts.

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:21.980
And that’s just one example, and not even
the most important one.

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:27.110
So, taking all of this into account, maybe
it would be good to consider creating a military

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:31.640
alliance?

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:32.640
...

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:40.670
A LATIN AMERICA NATO?

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:44.519
Allow me to propose an idea that may seem
a bit crazy.

00:10:44.519 --> 00:10:49.060
Based on what we just discussed, wouldn’t
it be good to think about building a great

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:50.190
military alliance?

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:54.399
An alliance in which different countries could
organize and provide different units and bodies

00:10:54.399 --> 00:10:56.250
for different tasks.

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.690
This would have several advantages:

00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:04.430
For one, military influence over political
power would decrease and sovereignty would

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:06.420
be guaranteed for all countries…

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:10.450
In addition, and if necessary, during natural
disasters, Latin American countries could

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:13.459
have an army that could deploy rapidly where
they are needed.

00:11:13.459 --> 00:11:18.350
It could also push for much needed Latin American
integration, and to increase this region’s

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:21.360
role in world politics.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:32.600
But, above all, it would free many resources,
both economic and human, to promote citizen

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:38.440
security and reduce violence rates in these
countries.

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:41.399
In a way the European Union is trying to do
something similar...

00:11:41.399 --> 00:11:43.420
so while it may sound like a bit of a crazy
idea…

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:45.759
Maybe it’s really not that crazy after all…

00:11:45.759 --> 00:11:50.279
For example, Costa Rica suppressed its army
decades ago and the results in terms of security

00:11:50.279 --> 00:11:52.390
have been better than those of its neighbors.

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:57.001
We’ve said before on VisualPolitik, that
the 21st century will be India’s Century

00:11:57.001 --> 00:12:07.910
(and probably China and Asia in general),
but now we wonder, why not Latin America too?

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:15.709
Now it’s your turn, do you agree with soldiers
replacing the police for ensuring security?

00:12:15.709 --> 00:12:29.920
Let us know in the comments below.

